WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:02.877
- My advice to young people
is you never want to do

00:00:02.877 --> 00:00:04.823
something on the spur of the moment.

00:00:04.823 --> 00:00:09.087
And if you say no, me me
me, I I I, nobody likes you.

00:00:09.087 --> 00:00:10.964
Anybody that tries to
centralize everything

00:00:10.964 --> 00:00:13.443
through themselves or through their staff

00:00:13.443 --> 00:00:15.211
really is making a terrible mistake

00:00:15.211 --> 00:00:17.273
You can't control how
lucky you are, although

00:00:17.273 --> 00:00:18.887
the more you work the luckier you get

00:00:18.887 --> 00:00:20.655
You can't just say
something and believe it

00:00:20.655 --> 00:00:24.161
You have to be able to show
people that you're right

00:00:24.161 --> 00:00:25.990
The most important asset
you have are the people

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:28.687
that leave at night and
come in in the morning

00:00:28.687 --> 00:00:30.617
I just don't have anything
in common with people

00:00:30.617 --> 00:00:32.983
that sit there and say "Oh
my God, it was terrible."

00:00:32.983 --> 00:00:34.782
You're going to spend
your entire life doing

00:00:34.782 --> 00:00:36.723
something different than what you think.

00:00:36.723 --> 00:00:39.168
Should've, would've and
could've is not a particularly

00:00:39.168 --> 00:00:42.299
useful thing to spend your time doing.

00:00:42.299 --> 00:00:44.715
This is a world, and I think it's good,

00:00:44.715 --> 00:00:47.051
where you don't do anything by yourself.

00:00:47.051 --> 00:00:49.315
- He's an American business
magnate, politician

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:50.555
and philanthropist.

00:00:50.555 --> 00:00:53.744
He served as the 108th mayor
of New York City serving

00:00:53.744 --> 00:00:56.419
for three consecutive
terms with a net worth

00:00:56.419 --> 00:00:59.668
of $41 billion, he's the
seventh richest person

00:00:59.668 --> 00:01:01.081
in the United States.

00:01:01.081 --> 00:01:02.422
He's Michael Bloomberg, and here are

00:01:02.422 --> 00:01:04.839
his top ten rules of success.

00:01:07.783 --> 00:01:10.284
- You know, I think it's very,
my advice to young people

00:01:10.284 --> 00:01:14.266
is you never want to do something
on the spur of the moment

00:01:14.266 --> 00:01:16.927
you don't want to jump too
quickly, that doesn't mean

00:01:16.927 --> 00:01:19.080
you should sit around
and waste your whole life

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.036
and never realize when
something's not going to happen

00:01:22.036 --> 00:01:24.364
or when the opportunity's
not going to be presented

00:01:24.364 --> 00:01:28.457
but every time I thought about
leaving Solomon I stopped

00:01:28.457 --> 00:01:31.728
and said you know I'm learning
a lot, and even though

00:01:31.728 --> 00:01:34.107
I might have been able to
make more money elsewhere

00:01:34.107 --> 00:01:35.437
it was a great firm.

00:01:35.437 --> 00:01:38.265
They were scrupulously honest,
phenomenally competent,

00:01:38.265 --> 00:01:42.265
worked very hard, very
nice to me, erm, and, er,

00:01:43.191 --> 00:01:45.495
I always decided to stay.

00:01:45.495 --> 00:01:48.616
I don't think I ever seriously,
other than maybe a little

00:01:48.616 --> 00:01:50.729
Walter Mitty dream now and
then, I don't think I ever

00:01:50.729 --> 00:01:54.321
seriously ever thought of
leaving and it turned out to be

00:01:54.321 --> 00:01:56.586
great, I'd say nothing but
good things about them,

00:01:56.586 --> 00:01:59.240
even when them firing
me was the right thing.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:02.723
- You never wanted to
beat your competition?

00:02:02.723 --> 00:02:04.827
- No, I wanted to be successful

00:02:04.827 --> 00:02:07.868
- You said teammates versus competition

00:02:07.868 --> 00:02:10.076
- But I mean, at Solomon
Brothers all those times,

00:02:10.076 --> 00:02:12.766
you work with other people,
one of the ways incidentally,

00:02:12.766 --> 00:02:15.536
Stephanie, to really get
ahead, is to give credit

00:02:15.536 --> 00:02:17.016
to somebody else.

00:02:17.016 --> 00:02:18.563
When I say oh, I didn't do it,

00:02:18.563 --> 00:02:22.303
Lloyd did that and he
involved me, let me tell you,

00:02:22.303 --> 00:02:25.799
I'm telling everybody it's
me as well, but I've now

00:02:25.799 --> 00:02:28.922
made a friend, and you
respect me because I've shared

00:02:28.922 --> 00:02:32.475
the credit, and if you
say, no, me me me, I I I,

00:02:32.475 --> 00:02:37.019
nobody likes you and nobody
respects you and they think

00:02:37.019 --> 00:02:40.648
you're exaggerating
everything, but to the extent

00:02:40.648 --> 00:02:42.508
you've credited someone
else with the thought

00:02:42.508 --> 00:02:44.909
of, are they exaggerating?

00:02:44.909 --> 00:02:46.476
It doesn't even come up.

00:02:46.476 --> 00:02:49.541
And I'm a big delegator in
terms of management style

00:02:49.541 --> 00:02:53.804
If you're going to run a 24/7 organization

00:02:53.804 --> 00:02:55.581
you have to do that.

00:02:55.581 --> 00:02:57.882
Anybody that tries to
centralize everything

00:02:57.882 --> 00:03:00.035
through themselves or through their staff

00:03:00.035 --> 00:03:01.958
really is making a terrible mistake.

00:03:01.958 --> 00:03:04.163
But that's why lots of
businesses don't work

00:03:04.163 --> 00:03:06.283
And that's why lots of
governments don't work

00:03:06.283 --> 00:03:08.002
Because they do exactly that.

00:03:08.002 --> 00:03:10.919
The only thing that you can control

00:03:12.737 --> 00:03:16.002
that influences success in life

00:03:16.002 --> 00:03:20.029
is how hard you work,
how honest you are, and

00:03:20.029 --> 00:03:22.391
how well you deal with others.

00:03:22.391 --> 00:03:25.058
You can control those variables.

00:03:26.376 --> 00:03:27.875
You can't control how lucky you are

00:03:27.875 --> 00:03:29.901
although the more you
work the luckier you get.

00:03:29.901 --> 00:03:33.688
You have no influence on the
intellectual capabilities

00:03:33.688 --> 00:03:37.088
that God did or did not
give you, but you can

00:03:37.088 --> 00:03:40.834
work as hard as possible, you
can be scrupulously honest

00:03:40.834 --> 00:03:44.091
so that people respect
you, and you can get along

00:03:44.091 --> 00:03:46.512
with others, cos nobody
does anything by themselves.

00:03:46.512 --> 00:03:49.120
- Did you have to pay a
price though, you personally?

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.610
- Well I think that
everything is an either-or.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:53.436
There's a price in everything.

00:03:53.436 --> 00:03:56.374
You can study or you can go out and play.

00:03:56.374 --> 00:04:00.776
You can defer to others or
you can try to hog the credit.

00:04:00.776 --> 00:04:05.065
You can be honest and
suffer the consequences,

00:04:05.065 --> 00:04:08.353
or you can lie, cheat and
steal, and short-term,

00:04:08.353 --> 00:04:09.673
maybe even advance.

00:04:09.673 --> 00:04:12.437
But long term, you are better
off if you work harder.

00:04:12.437 --> 00:04:14.407
You are better off if you're honest,

00:04:14.407 --> 00:04:16.528
and you are better off
if you give others credit

00:04:16.528 --> 00:04:18.814
and get them to help you by helping them.

00:04:18.814 --> 00:04:21.360
- What do you tell others who
try to strike that balance?

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.426
Between family and work
and volunteer efforts

00:04:24.426 --> 00:04:25.388
and anything else?

00:04:25.388 --> 00:04:27.927
- Well the answer to
either/or is invariably yes,

00:04:27.927 --> 00:04:29.810
you can't go all in one direction.

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:34.658
You cannot walk away from
your family for your career.

00:04:34.658 --> 00:04:37.332
On the other hand if you
walk away from your career

00:04:37.332 --> 00:04:39.824
for your family, you're not
going to have the money to

00:04:39.824 --> 00:04:42.421
support the family, and
parents all the time are making

00:04:42.421 --> 00:04:46.504
those kinds of choices
between career and family.

00:04:47.955 --> 00:04:51.647
They do some of both and
you try to blend the two,

00:04:51.647 --> 00:04:54.166
and that may not be the
perfect answer but that's a

00:04:54.166 --> 00:04:58.584
practical answer, that I've
always told young people

00:04:58.584 --> 00:05:01.527
when they go off to college,
you have to study and

00:05:01.527 --> 00:05:05.126
you have to get good
grades, but you certainly

00:05:05.126 --> 00:05:07.742
should have a good time as
well because that's part

00:05:07.742 --> 00:05:11.059
of your growing-up experience
and there's no point

00:05:11.059 --> 00:05:14.230
in leading life to suffer all the time,

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:17.482
you want to enjoy life as
well as the next person,

00:05:17.482 --> 00:05:20.651
so some balance, some
blend between the two.

00:05:20.651 --> 00:05:23.195
I went to Hopkins to study
physics and it turned

00:05:23.195 --> 00:05:26.103
out there was a German
requirement so after 30 days

00:05:26.103 --> 00:05:28.375
I became an engineer where
there was no language

00:05:28.375 --> 00:05:29.708
requirement.

00:05:29.708 --> 00:05:33.838
And I think looking back,
an engineering background

00:05:33.838 --> 00:05:37.522
was perhaps the transformative
thing in my life.

00:05:37.522 --> 00:05:41.362
Because in science, and it
would have worked if I had

00:05:41.362 --> 00:05:44.333
stayed as a physicist too,
I suppose, in science,

00:05:44.333 --> 00:05:48.446
you have to be able to
look in the mirror and

00:05:48.446 --> 00:05:50.988
answer the question, you
can't just say something

00:05:50.988 --> 00:05:53.455
and believe it, you have
to be able to show people

00:05:53.455 --> 00:05:55.179
that you're right.

00:05:55.179 --> 00:05:57.788
The essence of science
is that somebody else,

00:05:57.788 --> 00:05:59.279
independently, can verify

00:05:59.279 --> 00:06:01.102
what you claim you saw.

00:06:01.102 --> 00:06:04.269
And that discipline of not just taking

00:06:06.764 --> 00:06:10.882
people's word for it, or not
just automatically falling in

00:06:10.882 --> 00:06:15.049
to what's generally accepted,
has forced me to step back

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:17.263
and say why?

00:06:17.263 --> 00:06:18.852
And to question.

00:06:18.852 --> 00:06:22.045
And if you question
why we're doing things,

00:06:22.045 --> 00:06:27.006
I think you'll find yourself
targeting your energies

00:06:27.006 --> 00:06:29.907
much more efficiently,
appropriately, and in a much

00:06:29.907 --> 00:06:32.006
more satisfying way.

00:06:32.006 --> 00:06:34.039
So somebody says well this works,

00:06:34.039 --> 00:06:36.676
well that may be, but
you know, stop and think,

00:06:36.676 --> 00:06:39.099
does it make any common sense?

00:06:39.099 --> 00:06:42.766
Listen to what people
are telling you to do.

00:06:43.696 --> 00:06:47.076
Think about what they
say in the newspaper,

00:06:47.076 --> 00:06:49.481
does it make any sense?

00:06:49.481 --> 00:06:52.124
Do you want to be part of it?

00:06:52.124 --> 00:06:53.637
Could you do it better?

00:06:53.637 --> 00:06:57.220
It's that mental
discipline of questioning,

00:06:58.079 --> 00:07:00.645
which I guess is the scientific method.

00:07:00.645 --> 00:07:03.032
- What do you think has
mattered about the culture

00:07:03.032 --> 00:07:04.315
of Bloomberg?

00:07:04.315 --> 00:07:06.342
And what lessons do you
think a company like this

00:07:06.342 --> 00:07:07.575
should learn?

00:07:07.575 --> 00:07:09.115
- I think it's not just for companies

00:07:09.115 --> 00:07:10.189
I think it's for governments
and everybody else.

00:07:10.189 --> 00:07:13.946
People want recognition and respect.

00:07:13.946 --> 00:07:17.779
And whether you do it
by giving them free food

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:22.618
and nice offices, whether
you do it by always

00:07:22.618 --> 00:07:25.071
giving them the credit
when somebody says why

00:07:25.071 --> 00:07:26.267
does it go along?

00:07:26.267 --> 00:07:29.053
I always make a point when
I walk into a building

00:07:29.053 --> 00:07:32.722
I shake the hands of the
security people at the door.

00:07:32.722 --> 00:07:35.159
If it wasn't for them, the
rest of us wouldn't be able

00:07:35.159 --> 00:07:37.211
to go in and conduct our business.

00:07:37.211 --> 00:07:41.083
They're just as important
as the head of the company.

00:07:41.083 --> 00:07:43.902
I think we have too many
distinctions between

00:07:43.902 --> 00:07:46.831
people and the egalitarian
concept that America

00:07:46.831 --> 00:07:50.164
was built around, I think, really works.

00:07:52.737 --> 00:07:56.108
And at Bloomberg I abolished all titles

00:07:56.108 --> 00:07:58.141
there's no titles in the company.

00:07:58.141 --> 00:08:00.177
On my card, it never said anything,

00:08:00.177 --> 00:08:02.279
it just said Michael
Bloomberg, and address,

00:08:02.279 --> 00:08:03.661
and phone number.

00:08:03.661 --> 00:08:05.633
In those days you didn't
even have email addresses,

00:08:05.633 --> 00:08:08.800
today they continue with that process.

00:08:10.763 --> 00:08:12.764
Everybody knows who they work for,

00:08:12.764 --> 00:08:15.514
there is a hierarchy, you
have to have a hierarchy,

00:08:15.514 --> 00:08:17.878
you have to have structure,
but that doesn't mean

00:08:17.878 --> 00:08:22.045
that we should have, every
time we introduce somebody,

00:08:22.936 --> 00:08:25.629
something that distinguishes
and says that one person

00:08:25.629 --> 00:08:27.888
is more important than the other.

00:08:27.888 --> 00:08:30.630
In city government you can't quite do that

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:34.204
because they're built
into the structure is

00:08:34.204 --> 00:08:36.976
different, and the
requirements they use titles

00:08:36.976 --> 00:08:39.613
is different, but in the
private sector to the extent

00:08:39.613 --> 00:08:42.213
that you can include
everybody in this room and

00:08:42.213 --> 00:08:44.417
around the world that works
for Google, I would argue,

00:08:44.417 --> 00:08:46.306
do it, that's what we do at Bloomberg.

00:08:46.306 --> 00:08:50.500
To the extent that you
can make people be happy

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:52.512
to come to work and want to be there,

00:08:52.512 --> 00:08:55.334
in the end luck plays
a very important part

00:08:55.334 --> 00:08:58.143
in how successful you are,
but the harder you work,

00:08:58.143 --> 00:09:00.599
and the longer you work, the
luckier you're going to be.

00:09:00.599 --> 00:09:03.030
So you want people to want to
come in early in the morning

00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:04.450
and want to stay late.

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:07.154
You want people that, if they
want to take some time off

00:09:07.154 --> 00:09:09.206
for family obligations that's fine,

00:09:09.206 --> 00:09:11.074
but you want them to want to come back,

00:09:11.074 --> 00:09:14.371
and I think if you take a look,

00:09:14.371 --> 00:09:15.882
you're never going to please everybody.

00:09:15.882 --> 00:09:18.882
Bloomberg has about 9,500 employees,

00:09:20.024 --> 00:09:22.425
maybe 120 offices around the world,

00:09:22.425 --> 00:09:25.049
and there'll always be
somebody that isn't happy.

00:09:25.049 --> 00:09:28.166
But fundamentally we have
a very low turnover rate,

00:09:28.166 --> 00:09:31.857
and I think whenever you
have intellectual content

00:09:31.857 --> 00:09:36.737
as the basis of your business,
and certainly Google does,

00:09:36.737 --> 00:09:40.124
and certainly Bloomberg does,
the most important asset

00:09:40.124 --> 00:09:43.000
you have are the people that
leave at night and come in

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:44.190
in the morning.

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:45.889
And so that's where your focus should be.

00:09:45.889 --> 00:09:47.597
- And what's your advice to these small

00:09:47.597 --> 00:09:49.588
business founders today?

00:09:49.588 --> 00:09:52.063
- Work hard, you're
going to need some luck,

00:09:52.063 --> 00:09:53.909
but the harder you work,
the luckier you get.

00:09:53.909 --> 00:09:56.978
And if it doesn't work out,
it's not the end of the world.

00:09:56.978 --> 00:09:59.799
I just don't have anything
in common with people

00:09:59.799 --> 00:10:02.019
that sit there and say "Oh
my God, it was terrible."

00:10:02.019 --> 00:10:05.974
It's water over the dam, under
the bridge, get on with it

00:10:05.974 --> 00:10:08.438
and start another business the next day,

00:10:08.438 --> 00:10:11.249
or another career, and be
honest, look in the mirror,

00:10:11.249 --> 00:10:12.831
and say why didn't it work out?

00:10:12.831 --> 00:10:15.989
Well it wasn't the skillset,
or it wasn't I didn't have

00:10:15.989 --> 00:10:18.118
the temperament for it, or
I was in the wrong place,

00:10:18.118 --> 00:10:21.092
wrong time, maybe I can
afford to do it again,

00:10:21.092 --> 00:10:23.914
maybe I can't, but people
aren't honest with themselves.

00:10:23.914 --> 00:10:26.267
What I've always done is
I've taken a yellow pad,

00:10:26.267 --> 00:10:27.879
I've drawn a line down the middle,

00:10:27.879 --> 00:10:30.280
write out the pros on one
side, the cons on another,

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:33.205
in the sense that if I let
you see it, you wouldn't

00:10:33.205 --> 00:10:34.080
laugh at me.

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:36.090
And then when I get done, I
just rip it up and throw it

00:10:36.090 --> 00:10:38.503
away because I've worked out in my mind,

00:10:38.503 --> 00:10:41.789
What is real, and what
isn't and what I can

00:10:41.789 --> 00:10:44.284
present to people and what I can't.

00:10:44.284 --> 00:10:49.016
And this goes back to my boy
scout days, is be prepared.

00:10:49.016 --> 00:10:50.902
I know what you're thinking, you're

00:10:50.902 --> 00:10:53.757
already prepared, you've mapped
out the rest of your life,

00:10:53.757 --> 00:10:56.688
the rest of your education,
postgraduate work,

00:10:56.688 --> 00:11:00.771
residencies, fellowships,
clerk ships, and you're

00:11:02.471 --> 00:11:04.627
going to have a long, meaningful career.

00:11:04.627 --> 00:11:07.303
You have all of your
plans already laid down.

00:11:07.303 --> 00:11:10.305
That's not the preparation
I'm talking about,

00:11:10.305 --> 00:11:13.717
because let me tell you
the likelihood of you

00:11:13.717 --> 00:11:16.093
winding up 10 years from
now doing what you think

00:11:16.093 --> 00:11:17.862
you're going to be
doing ten years from now

00:11:17.862 --> 00:11:19.991
is probably less than one in ten.

00:11:19.991 --> 00:11:22.028
The truth of the matter is, you don't know

00:11:22.028 --> 00:11:24.218
what opportunities are
going to be presented

00:11:24.218 --> 00:11:27.041
to you, you don't know where
you're going to succeed

00:11:27.041 --> 00:11:29.922
and where you're going
to fail, the careers that

00:11:29.922 --> 00:11:32.413
you wind up in are invariably
going to be very different

00:11:32.413 --> 00:11:36.580
so what I would do is I
would focus on how I interact

00:11:37.423 --> 00:11:41.115
with people, how I approach
problems, I would get involved

00:11:41.115 --> 00:11:44.042
in government, if you want
to go into public service,

00:11:44.042 --> 00:11:46.611
I would recommend you first
become a billionaire but

00:11:46.611 --> 00:11:48.361
that's another issue.

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:53.238
You're going to spend your
entire life doing something

00:11:53.238 --> 00:11:55.681
different than what you
think, and the good news is

00:11:55.681 --> 00:11:58.288
that Dartmouth is preparing
you for that, because

00:11:58.288 --> 00:12:00.338
while you think there are
individual subjects, what

00:12:00.338 --> 00:12:02.747
they're really teaching you
to do is to think and to

00:12:02.747 --> 00:12:05.710
reason and to build relationships,
and that's what's going

00:12:05.710 --> 00:12:07.005
to carry you.

00:12:07.005 --> 00:12:10.346
- Well from the book, after
you were fired or let go

00:12:10.346 --> 00:12:12.702
from Solomon, "I didn't sit
around wondering what was

00:12:12.702 --> 00:12:14.776
"happening at the old firm,
I didn't go back and visit,

00:12:14.776 --> 00:12:18.083
"I never look over my
shoulder, once finished, gone,

00:12:18.083 --> 00:12:19.400
"life continues."

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:21.841
Yes, you can't sit there,
and should've, would've

00:12:21.841 --> 00:12:24.467
and could've is not a
particularly useful thing

00:12:24.467 --> 00:12:26.792
to spend your time doing.

00:12:26.792 --> 00:12:28.292
It will just leave you

00:12:28.292 --> 00:12:31.788
heartsick, or it'll leave
you living a past life

00:12:31.788 --> 00:12:34.681
and not looking towards what
could be great tomorrow.

00:12:34.681 --> 00:12:37.605
- How important is it
to have salesmanship,

00:12:37.605 --> 00:12:39.610
you could have the best idea in the world?

00:12:39.610 --> 00:12:41.814
- I don't know any business
where sales isn't a big

00:12:41.814 --> 00:12:44.899
part of it, or cooperation
and collaboration isn't

00:12:44.899 --> 00:12:47.658
a big part of it, I suppose
if you're a monk and have

00:12:47.658 --> 00:12:50.745
a vow of silence, maybe,
but an author, for example,

00:12:50.745 --> 00:12:53.185
you've got to work with an
editor, you've got to talk

00:12:53.185 --> 00:12:55.457
to a publisher, and you have
to deal with your family.

00:12:55.457 --> 00:13:00.363
And it's, they're all, this
is a world, and I think

00:13:00.363 --> 00:13:03.177
it's good, where you don't
do anything by yourself.

00:13:03.177 --> 00:13:06.384
Nobody just works for one
person or just works for

00:13:06.384 --> 00:13:09.445
themselves, nobody does
anything independently,

00:13:09.445 --> 00:13:13.612
we deal, and whether it's
your family, your outside

00:13:14.926 --> 00:13:18.009
life, your business life, or whatever

00:13:20.428 --> 00:13:22.102
- Thank you guys so much
for watching, I made

00:13:22.102 --> 00:13:25.038
this video because Will C
asked me to, so if there's

00:13:25.038 --> 00:13:27.316
a famous entrepreneur that
you want me to profile next,

00:13:27.316 --> 00:13:29.562
leave it in the comments
below, I'll see what I can do.

00:13:29.562 --> 00:13:31.715
I also love to know which
of Michael Bloomberg's

00:13:31.715 --> 00:13:34.598
top ten rules meant the
most to you, what's going to

00:13:34.598 --> 00:13:36.887
have the biggest impact on
your life, on your business,

00:13:36.887 --> 00:13:38.673
leave it in the comments
during the discussion.

00:13:38.673 --> 00:13:40.312
Thank you guys so much
for watching, continue

00:13:40.312 --> 00:13:43.145
to believe, and I'll see you soon.

00:13:49.163 --> 00:13:51.233
- What would you like
to see as your legacy?

00:13:51.233 --> 00:13:53.752
- What would you like to
say that you achieved with

00:13:53.752 --> 00:13:55.264
your life?

00:13:55.264 --> 00:13:57.966
- Well I think I'd want my
kids to look back and say

00:13:57.966 --> 00:14:02.222
he was a great mayor, and
honest, and made a big

00:14:02.222 --> 00:14:04.521
difference in the city,
but I think the things

00:14:04.521 --> 00:14:08.688
that will last are things
like the life expectancy

00:14:10.404 --> 00:14:14.251
numbers or the economic
development of New York City

00:14:14.251 --> 00:14:17.884
compared to other cities,
or reforming education,

00:14:17.884 --> 00:14:20.799
which is always a two-step
forward, one-step back thing,

00:14:20.799 --> 00:14:24.605
but if we can be an impetus
to Rahm Emanuel, for example,

00:14:24.605 --> 00:14:27.921
to stand up and fight for
the control of the school

00:14:27.921 --> 00:14:31.219
system in Chicago, or
elsewhere around the country,

00:14:31.219 --> 00:14:33.890
I think those would be good legacies.

00:14:33.890 --> 00:14:35.964
If we could figure out a
way to stop this carnage

00:14:35.964 --> 00:14:39.259
from illegal guns, that
would be another thing,

00:14:39.259 --> 00:14:42.681
but if you go back, go down
the road and look back,

00:14:42.681 --> 00:14:46.666
those are the kinds of things,
investment in educational

00:14:46.666 --> 00:14:50.413
institutions, my alma mater,
Johns Hopkins, that you're

00:14:50.413 --> 00:14:54.206
on the board of, or Harvard,
where one of my daughters

00:14:54.206 --> 00:14:57.482
went to graduate school,
and I did, or Princeton,

00:14:57.482 --> 00:15:00.344
and NYU, those universities will, I hope,

00:15:00.344 --> 00:15:03.171
make a difference in the
world and I'll be a little

00:15:03.171 --> 00:15:04.482
part of it.

00:15:04.482 --> 00:15:07.145
At Hopkins we have a malaria
project, for example,

00:15:07.145 --> 00:15:09.309
trying to build a better mosquito.

00:15:09.309 --> 00:15:11.991
And the reason, you want
a mosquito that's tougher

00:15:11.991 --> 00:15:14.290
than the others that
doesn't carry the malaria

00:15:14.290 --> 00:15:16.735
parasite, and then it
would drive out the others.

00:15:16.735 --> 00:15:19.299
It's really probably the
only ways to stop malaria

00:15:19.299 --> 00:15:21.895
which kills a couple of
million people every year.

00:15:21.895 --> 00:15:25.895
When our Nobel Prize
winner who runs the project

00:15:27.084 --> 00:15:29.979
wins his second Nobel Prize,
and I've challenged him

00:15:29.979 --> 00:15:33.002
to do so, I'll have a
little bit of that prize,

00:15:33.002 --> 00:15:36.156
'cause I helped fund it,
and so will the clinicians,

00:15:36.156 --> 00:15:37.998
and other scientists that work with him,

00:15:37.998 --> 00:15:39.868
and so will patients that work with him.

00:15:39.868 --> 00:15:43.957
We don't do anything on
our own, and everybody

00:15:43.957 --> 00:15:47.282
collaborates and gets
something from somebody else.

00:15:47.282 --> 00:15:49.789
And I also hope that I'll
set a standard and that

00:15:49.789 --> 00:15:53.534
whoever comes after me will
be a better mayor than I am.

00:15:53.534 --> 00:15:57.109
I'm going to live in New York
City and I've always, in my life

00:15:57.109 --> 00:15:59.988
tried to hire people smarter than me.

00:15:59.988 --> 00:16:03.257
I've certainly done that with
my deputy mayors in the city,

00:16:03.257 --> 00:16:05.201
I've certainly done that with
the people that are running

00:16:05.201 --> 00:16:09.048
Bloomberg, and hopefully the
public will pick a successor

00:16:09.048 --> 00:16:12.962
that will turn out to be,
nobody'll ever think anybody is

00:16:12.962 --> 00:16:16.901
at the beginning, but will
turn out to be a better mayor

00:16:16.901 --> 00:16:18.933
and will take it forward.

00:16:18.933 --> 00:16:21.300
And I hope that Washington
gets its act together

00:16:21.300 --> 00:16:23.735
and starts understanding,
we are in trouble

00:16:23.735 --> 00:16:25.300
in this country.

00:16:25.300 --> 00:16:27.914
Niall Ferguson wrote a
great story about the end

00:16:27.914 --> 00:16:31.381
of civilizations and how
quickly things can collapse.

00:16:31.381 --> 00:16:35.364
Rome in a couple of decades,
the USSR in a couple of years.

00:16:35.364 --> 00:16:38.079
I mean that was the second
biggest power in the world

00:16:38.079 --> 00:16:40.141
and two years, it's gone.

00:16:40.141 --> 00:16:45.040
We really are on a cliff, and
it's not just the fiscal cliff

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:48.176
we're already over the fiscal
cliff, the question is,

00:16:48.176 --> 00:16:50.366
are we going to pull a rip
cord or just keep falling?

00:16:50.366 --> 00:16:53.488
But we're on a cliff socially, as well.

00:16:53.488 --> 00:16:56.517
And the world is not
necessarily on our side.

00:16:56.517 --> 00:16:58.780
- Did you ever think your
company wasn't going to make it,

00:16:58.780 --> 00:17:00.028
in the early years?

00:17:00.028 --> 00:17:01.507
Were you nervous it
wasn't going to make it?

00:17:01.507 --> 00:17:02.400
- Sure.

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:05.054
For the first year,
it's always an exciting

00:17:05.054 --> 00:17:08.028
thing, every two weeks
I wrote another check

00:17:08.028 --> 00:17:10.233
to the company from my
personal bank account,

00:17:10.233 --> 00:17:11.763
to put money in to pay the help.

00:17:11.763 --> 00:17:14.510
And the last year of the
three that it took me

00:17:14.510 --> 00:17:17.196
to get customers, you
could see that that light

00:17:17.196 --> 00:17:18.940
at the end of the tunnel wasn't a train

00:17:18.940 --> 00:17:21.520
coming towards you, it
really was that you were

00:17:21.520 --> 00:17:23.131
going to get out of this
and it was going to work.

00:17:23.131 --> 00:17:25.618
The middle year, what you
find is that your family

00:17:25.618 --> 00:17:28.030
and your friends say,
you know, I know you know

00:17:28.030 --> 00:17:30.706
what you're doing and I have
all that confidence in ya,

00:17:30.706 --> 00:17:34.522
but you know just in case,
because we were competing

00:17:34.522 --> 00:17:37.197
against big companies, and
every article in the paper

00:17:37.197 --> 00:17:39.743
said this was foolish, you
can't win against big companies,

00:17:39.743 --> 00:17:42.222
I've always thought that
competing against big companies

00:17:42.222 --> 00:17:46.395
for a small company is not
only exciting, it is, it has

00:17:46.395 --> 00:17:49.256
enormous potential, because
big companies never believe

00:17:49.256 --> 00:17:51.785
the little company is going
to challenge them until

00:17:51.785 --> 00:17:54.537
it's too late, and big companies
have such an infrastructure

00:17:54.537 --> 00:17:57.218
and investment, and product
and people and brands, and

00:17:57.218 --> 00:17:59.640
that sort of thing, that they
can't change, and you know,

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:02.760
Clay Christensen's Disruptive
Technologies, if you need

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:06.857
more examples of that taking
place just pick up the paper.

00:18:06.857 --> 00:18:09.877
Every day there's another
industry going out of business.

00:18:09.877 --> 00:18:12.889
The other day we gave
awards to ten different

00:18:12.889 --> 00:18:15.570
small startup companies.

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:17.460
Our economic development corporation did.

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:18.816
In the fashion industry.

00:18:18.816 --> 00:18:20.942
And I'm talking to this guy,
what did he get an award from?

00:18:20.942 --> 00:18:23.678
He has a computer program,
you just stand in front

00:18:23.678 --> 00:18:26.557
of his camera, turn around
once, and then he has your

00:18:26.557 --> 00:18:29.846
body measurements for every
single square inch of you

00:18:29.846 --> 00:18:31.261
down to a science.

00:18:31.261 --> 00:18:32.838
That goes straight to the loom,

00:18:32.838 --> 00:18:35.717
and the next set of jeans
that comes off is built

00:18:35.717 --> 00:18:37.876
perfectly for your body.

00:18:37.876 --> 00:18:39.487
Think about how many people in the process

00:18:39.487 --> 00:18:40.714
that's going to take out.

00:18:40.714 --> 00:18:43.319
That's one of the great
challenges for creating new jobs

00:18:43.319 --> 00:18:46.562
that that kind of technology
takes away a lot of the

00:18:46.562 --> 00:18:47.709
kinds of things.

00:18:47.709 --> 00:18:50.480
But there is still the demand
for people creating that

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:53.434
kind of program, there's
still demand for the designer

00:18:53.434 --> 00:18:56.561
who comes up with what's
fashionable or not.

00:18:56.561 --> 00:19:00.586
So there is potential, but
it requires investment in

00:19:00.586 --> 00:19:03.145
infrastructure and it
requires better education

00:19:03.145 --> 00:19:07.219
than we are providing our kids today with.

00:19:07.219 --> 00:19:10.091
And it requires people from
around the world wanting to

00:19:10.091 --> 00:19:12.797
come here, rather than sending
these industries overseas.

